**What**

`--reporter`, `--assignee`, `--reviewer`, `--requester` and `--label` are all
repeatable free-form strings with no validation, no enum, no per-repo
declaration and no rename path. Meanwhile every write is a Git commit carrying
a real author and committer identity. The tool has two identity systems and
says nothing about which is authoritative.

**Where**

- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:91` -- `labels`, `assignees`, `reporters` as `Vec<String>`
- `crates/gix-forge/src/lib.rs:77` -- the same on the entity
- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:495` -- `known_issue_values`, which recovers a
  de-facto vocabulary by scanning every existing entity

**Evidence**

The commit that records an issue is authored by the Git identity:

    author=Tester <t@example.com>
    committer=Tester <t@example.com>

The `reporters` field on that same issue is whatever string was passed. Nothing
connects them and nothing checks them. Any user can file an issue reported by
anyone. Filing these issues, I was asked to pass
`--reporter Claude:claude-opus-5`, a `Name:model` convention that also appears
in `.rules` for the `Assisted-by` commit trailer -- so the convention is real
and used, and it is documented only in a file about how to instruct agents.

`known_issue_values` is the tell. It exists because there is no vocabulary to
read, so the interactive picker reconstructs one by loading every issue in the
repository and unioning the strings it finds. That is a repo-wide scan standing
in for a config file, and it means a typo becomes a permanent menu entry.

**Why it matters**

Two consequences, one practical and one thematic.

Practically: labels drift immediately (`bug`, `Bug`, `bugs` are three labels),
assignees cannot be validated against anyone real, and there is no rename, so
the taxonomy of a busy repository degrades and cannot be repaired.

Thematically, this is the composition the family is set up to make and does not
make. `git-store` has schemas. `git-query` has repo-stored rules and already
defines `member` and `revoked` EDB predicates backed by `refs/meta/members/*`.
The obvious shape is right there: labels are a schema, members are the existing
member namespace, and policy about them -- which labels are legal, who may
close, whether an assignee must be a member -- is a `git-query` rule. Instead
each is an unvalidated string and the member namespace goes unused. A reviewer
in the audience who knows the sibling projects will spot this immediately,
because it is the join the four-project story implies.

**Options**

- Option A -- Declare the vocabulary as a stored entity kind of its own, so
  labels are data in the repo with their own schema and history and renames are
  ordinary edits. Trade-off: one more kind, and `new` has to validate against it.
- Option B -- Make people first-class: resolve `--assignee` and `--reporter`
  against `refs/meta/members/*`, the namespace `git-query` already reads, and
  fall back to the Git committer identity when omitted. Trade-off: requires the
  member namespace to be populated, which is currently nobody`s job.
- Option C -- Leave the fields free-form but express the policy as `git-query`
  rules that `git-forge` checks on write, so the taxonomy is advisory and
  enforceable without being hardcoded. Trade-off: the most in keeping with the
  thesis and the most moving parts.

**Recommendation**

B first, because defaulting the reporter to the Git identity removes the
duplicate-identity question entirely and costs almost nothing -- and note that
a free-form reporter is forgeable while a committer identity is at least
signable. Then C, because a demo where a Datalog rule rejects an issue with an
undeclared label would land the whole family thesis in about fifteen seconds.
Medium-high confidence on B, and C is genuinely a judgement call about how much
machinery a bug tracker should have.